export const notes = "<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>1. Why Classify Living Things?</h3>\n  <p>With millions of species on Earth, classification makes organisms easier to <strong>study, identify, and communicate about</strong> across languages and cultures.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Taxonomy</strong> = the science of classifying living things</li>\n    <li><strong>Binomial nomenclature</strong>: every organism has a two-part scientific name \u2014 <em>Genus species</em></li>\n    <li>Rules: Genus is <strong>capitalised</strong>, species is <strong>lowercase</strong>; written in <em>italics</em> or underlined</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"example\">Examples: <em>Homo sapiens</em> (human), <em>Panthera leo</em> (lion), <em>Felis catus</em> (cat)</div>\n  <p><strong>Classification hierarchy</strong> (broadest \u2192 narrowest):</p>\n  <p style=\"text-align:center;font-weight:bold;\">Kingdom \u2192 Phylum \u2192 Class \u2192 Order \u2192 Family \u2192 Genus \u2192 Species</p>\n  <div class=\"example\">Mnemonic: <strong>\"King Philip Came Over For Good Soup\"</strong></div>\n  <p><strong>Species definition</strong>: a group of organisms that can interbreed with each other to produce <em>fertile offspring</em>.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>2. The Five Kingdoms</h3>\n  <table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.9em;\">\n    <thead><tr style=\"background:#e8f4f8;\">\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Kingdom</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Cell type</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Cell wall</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Nutrition</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Examples</th>\n    </tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Monera</strong> (Bacteria)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Prokaryotic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes (peptidoglycan)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Autotrophic or heterotrophic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><em>E. coli</em>, <em>Streptococcus</em></td></tr>\n      <tr style=\"background:#f9f9f9;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Protista</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Eukaryotic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Some (cellulose)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Autotrophic or heterotrophic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Amoeba, Paramecium, Euglena, algae</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Fungi</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Eukaryotic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes (chitin)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Heterotrophic (saprophytic)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Mushrooms, yeast, bread mould</td></tr>\n      <tr style=\"background:#f9f9f9;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Plantae</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Eukaryotic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes (cellulose)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Autotrophic (photosynthesis)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Mosses, ferns, flowering plants</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Animalia</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Eukaryotic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Heterotrophic</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Insects, fish, mammals</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"example\">Key distinction: Only Monera are <strong>prokaryotic</strong> (no nucleus). All others are eukaryotic.</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>3. Kingdom Animalia \u2014 Vertebrates vs Invertebrates</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Vertebrates</strong>: have a backbone (vertebral column); ~50,000 species (~5% of all animals)</li>\n    <li><strong>Invertebrates</strong>: no backbone; ~95% of all animal species (insects, worms, jellyfish, molluscs, crustaceans)</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><strong>5 Classes of Vertebrates:</strong></p>\n  <table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.85em;\">\n    <thead><tr style=\"background:#e8f4f8;\">\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Class</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Skin</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Temp. Regulation</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Reproduction</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Breathing</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Examples</th>\n    </tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\"><strong>Fish</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Scales</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Ectotherm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Eggs in water</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Gills</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Salmon, shark, goldfish</td></tr>\n      <tr style=\"background:#f9f9f9;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\"><strong>Amphibians</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Moist, smooth</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Ectotherm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Eggs in water; tadpole larva</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Gills (larva), lungs+skin (adult)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Frog, toad, salamander</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\"><strong>Reptiles</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Dry scales</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Ectotherm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Eggs on land (leathery shell)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Lungs</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Snake, lizard, crocodile, turtle</td></tr>\n      <tr style=\"background:#f9f9f9;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\"><strong>Birds</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Feathers</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Endotherm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Eggs on land (hard shell)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Lungs</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Eagle, penguin, parrot</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\"><strong>Mammals</strong></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Hair/fur</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Endotherm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Live young (mostly); nurse with milk</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Lungs</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:5px;\">Dog, whale, bat, human</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <div class=\"example\"><strong>Ectotherm</strong> = body temperature depends on environment (fish, amphibians, reptiles). <strong>Endotherm</strong> = maintains constant body temperature internally (birds, mammals).</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>4. Kingdom Plantae \u2014 Plant Groups</h3>\n  <table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:0.9em;\">\n    <thead><tr style=\"background:#e8f4f8;\">\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Group</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Seeds?</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Flowers?</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Vascular tissue?</th>\n      <th style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Examples</th>\n    </tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Mosses</strong> (Bryophytes)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Moss, liverwort</td></tr>\n      <tr style=\"background:#f9f9f9;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Ferns</strong> (Pteridophytes)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No (spores)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Fern, horsetail</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Conifers</strong> (Gymnosperms)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes (naked, in cones)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">No</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Pine, spruce, cedar</td></tr>\n      <tr style=\"background:#f9f9f9;\"><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\"><strong>Flowering plants</strong> (Angiosperms)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes (in fruit)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Yes</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #ccc;padding:6px;\">Rose, apple, grass, orchid</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n  <p><strong>Monocots vs Dicots</strong> (within angiosperms):</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Monocots</strong>: 1 seed leaf (cotyledon), parallel leaf veins, petals in multiples of 3 \u2014 e.g., grass, orchid, lily</li>\n    <li><strong>Dicots</strong>: 2 seed leaves, network (branching) leaf veins, petals in multiples of 4 or 5 \u2014 e.g., rose, bean, sunflower</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"example\">Memory tip: <strong>Mono</strong>cot = <strong>1</strong> cotyledon; <strong>Di</strong>cot = <strong>2</strong> cotyledons</div>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>5. Kingdom Fungi</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Not plants</strong>: cannot photosynthesise; no chlorophyll</li>\n    <li>Cell wall made of <strong>chitin</strong> (not cellulose like plants)</li>\n    <li><strong>Saprophytic nutrition</strong>: secrete enzymes onto dead organic matter \u2192 absorb digested nutrients</li>\n    <li>Body = <strong>hyphae</strong> (thread-like filaments); mass of hyphae = <strong>mycelium</strong></li>\n    <li>Reproduction: mainly by <strong>spores</strong></li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Mushrooms (<em>Agaricus</em>) \u2014 multicellular</li>\n    <li>Yeast \u2014 <strong>unicellular</strong>; used in bread-making and brewing beer</li>\n    <li>Bread mould (<em>Rhizopus</em>) \u2014 grows on stale bread</li>\n    <li><em>Penicillium</em> \u2014 source of the antibiotic penicillin</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><strong>Importance of Fungi:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Decomposers \u2014 recycle nutrients back into the ecosystem</li>\n    <li>Food production \u2014 bread, cheese, beer, soy sauce</li>\n    <li>Medicine \u2014 antibiotics (penicillin from <em>Penicillium</em>)</li>\n    <li>Some are harmful \u2014 athlete's foot, ringworm, food spoilage</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>6. Dichotomous Keys</h3>\n  <p>A <strong>dichotomous key</strong> is a tool used to identify unknown organisms by answering a series of yes/no (or either/or) questions about observable features.</p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>\"Dichotomous\" = divides into <strong>two</strong> choices at each step</li>\n    <li><strong>How to use</strong>: Start at question 1 \u2192 choose the statement that matches \u2192 follow to the next question \u2192 repeat until the organism is identified</li>\n    <li><strong>How to construct</strong>: Choose ONE feature that divides the group in two \u2192 write two contrasting statements \u2192 subdivide each group further</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><strong>Good features to use:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Has legs / No legs</li>\n    <li>Has wings / No wings</li>\n    <li>Has feathers / Has scales</li>\n    <li>Warm-blooded / Cold-blooded</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"example\"><strong>Tip</strong>: Always use <em>observable, objective</em> features \u2014 avoid subjective terms like \"large\" or \"beautiful\". A good key leads to only ONE possible answer for each organism.</div>\n  <p><strong>Practice</strong>: Given 4 animals, construct a key using 2\u20133 questions that correctly identifies each one.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"notes-card\">\n  <h3>7. Endangered Species and Singapore Biodiversity</h3>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Extinction</strong>: permanent disappearance of a species from Earth</li>\n    <li><strong>IUCN Red List</strong>: international classification of conservation status</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p style=\"text-align:center;\">Extinct (EX) \u2192 Extinct in Wild (EW) \u2192 Critically Endangered (CR) \u2192 Endangered (EN) \u2192 Vulnerable (VU) \u2192 Near Threatened (NT) \u2192 Least Concern (LC)</p>\n  <p><strong>Causes of species decline:</strong> habitat destruction, overhunting, invasive species, pollution, climate change</p>\n  <p><strong>Singapore examples:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Singapore freshwater crab</strong> (<em>Johora singaporensis</em>): critically endangered; found only in Bukit Timah Nature Reserve streams</li>\n    <li><strong>Raffles' Banded Langur</strong>: only ~60 individuals remaining in Singapore</li>\n    <li><strong>Sunda Pangolin</strong>: critically endangered; most trafficked mammal in the world</li>\n    <li><strong>Raffles' pitcher plant</strong> (<em>Nepenthes rafflesiana</em>): carnivorous plant found in Singapore</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p><strong>Why biodiversity matters:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Ecosystem services (clean air, water, pollination)</li>\n    <li>Medicines \u2014 many drugs derived from wild species</li>\n    <li>Food security \u2014 genetic diversity in crops</li>\n    <li>Intrinsic value \u2014 all species have a right to exist</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>";

